Hi and thanks @dgozman :)
But issue is not about test timeouts, it's about reported test durations; i.e. my tests are not hitting timeouts, it's _just_ that the runner is attributing the duration of the fixture set up to the first test handled by runner afterwards. Code for my minimal example on description here:

```ts
import { test as baseTest } from "@playwright/test";

const wait = (m: number) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, m));

const test = baseTest.extend<
  {},
  {
    workerFixture: any;
  }
>({
  workerFixture: [
    async ({}, use) => {
      console.log("wait for 20 seconds");
      await wait(20000);
      await use({});
    },
    { scope: "worker" },
  ],
});

test("wait for five seconds, 1", async ({ workerFixture }) => {
  console.log("wait for 5 seconds");
  await wait(5000);
});
test("wait for five seconds, 2", async ({ workerFixture }) => {
  console.log("wait for 5 seconds");
  await wait(5000);
});
test("wait for five seconds, 3", async ({ workerFixture }) => {
  console.log("wait for 5 seconds");
  await wait(5000);
});
```
Output:
```
Running 3 tests using 1 worker

  ✓  tests/test.issue.spec.ts:21:1 › wait for five seconds, 1 (25s)
wait for 20 seconds
wait for 5 seconds
  ✓  tests/test.issue.spec.ts:25:1 › wait for five seconds, 2 (5s)
wait for 5 seconds
  ✓  tests/test.issue.spec.ts:29:1 › wait for five seconds, 3 (5s)
wait for 5 seconds
```

Again, not a nag as this skews the stats as described on summary. I could have a short test once in a while reporting as taking way longer, thus I lose visibility on which tests I need to optimise. Thanks again and have a nice day, cheers.
